      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contribution to Science and Education</strong><br>\nApart from his research, Libby took on various educational roles, including a professorship at the University of Chicago. He was dedicated to teaching and mentoring the next generation of scientists. His commitment to education was as significant as his research contributions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>WWII and the Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Libby worked on the Manhattan Project, contributing to the development of the atomic bomb. His expertise in radioactive isotopes was crucial in the project's pursuit of nuclear fission to create weapons of mass destruction.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-War Efforts and Atmospheric Studies</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Libby's focus shifted to understanding the natural environment. He conducted research on the movement of tritium and carbon-14 through the Earth's atmosphere and biosphere, which was critical in studying atmospheric processes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
