          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Archaeology</strong><br>\nLibby's radiocarbon dating method revolutionized archaeology by providing a reliable dating tool. It enabled archaeologists to put historical artifacts into a precise chronological order, transforming the study of human history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Libby's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Libby continued to work on radiocarbon dating and furthered his studies in environmental science. He served as the director of the Institute for Geophysics at the University of California, Los Angeles, until his retirement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nWillard Libby passed away on September 8, 1980. His legacy lives on through the continued use of radiocarbon dating in science. Today, his method is still a fundamental tool for dating archaeological finds, understanding climate change, and tracing carbon through various ecological systems.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
